= Wave 11 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

Wave 11 extends prior cryptography, distributed systems, identity, verification, governance, incentives, and systems concepts into decentralized technology and market primitives.

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Hash function ===
A hash function maps input data of arbitrary or variable length to a fixed-size or bounded-size output called a hash value or digest.

=== Public-key cryptography ===
Public-key cryptography uses related public and private keys to perform complementary cryptographic operations without requiring every interacting party to share the same secret key in advance.

=== Digital signature ===
A digital signature is a cryptographic value generated with a private signing key that allows a verifier with the corresponding public key to check the integrity and claimed origin of signed data.

=== Blockchain ===
A blockchain is a distributed digital ledger in which validated records are grouped into ordered blocks that are cryptographically linked and replicated across a network according to established consensus rules.

=== Consensus mechanism ===
A consensus mechanism is a protocol or rule set by which participants in a distributed system establish agreement on a valid shared state or ordered history despite failures, delay, or adversarial behavior within stated assumptions.

=== Smart contract ===
A smart contract is program code and persistent state deployed to a distributed ledger or blockchain and executed according to that system's transaction and consensus rules.

=== Token (blockchain) ===
A blockchain token is a ledger-managed representation of an asset, unit, claim, access right, or other transferable or account-associated state defined by a blockchain protocol or smart contract.

=== Cryptocurrency wallet ===
A cryptocurrency wallet is hardware, software, or a service that generates, stores, manages, or uses cryptographic keys and addresses needed to authorize interactions with cryptocurrency or blockchain systems.

=== Decentralized identity ===
Decentralized identity is an identity architecture in which subjects or controllers can establish and use identifiers and verifiable claims without depending on one central identity provider for every interaction.

=== Zero-knowledge proof ===
A zero-knowledge proof is a cryptographic proof system in which a prover convinces a verifier that a statement is true while revealing no information beyond what the protocol permits about the witness or secret used to establish that statement.

=== Decentralized finance ===
Decentralized finance (DeFi) is a family of financial applications and protocols that use distributed ledgers and smart contracts to provide functions such as trading, lending, borrowing, asset management, and settlement.

=== Automated market maker ===
An automated market maker (AMM) is a rule-based market mechanism that quotes or executes trades algorithmically against on-chain or otherwise pooled liquidity rather than requiring each trade to match a contemporaneous opposing order.

=== Liquidity pool ===
A liquidity pool is a set of assets committed under shared rules so that participants or protocols can draw on those assets for trading, lending, settlement, or other liquidity-demanding operations.

=== Liquidity provider ===
A liquidity provider is a participant that commits assets or capital to a market or protocol so that other participants can trade, borrow, settle, or otherwise transact against available liquidity.

=== Constant-product market maker ===
A constant-product market maker is an automated market maker whose basic two-asset reserve rule maintains a product relation of the form x·y=k, subject to fees and implementation details.

=== Concentrated liquidity ===
Concentrated liquidity is a market-making design in which liquidity providers allocate capital to selected price intervals rather than supplying the same liquidity across the entire possible price range.

=== Arbitrage ===
Arbitrage is a trading strategy that seeks to profit from inconsistent prices or economically equivalent payoffs across markets, instruments, or states while minimizing or hedging net exposure to the underlying price movement.

=== Slippage ===
Slippage is the difference between an expected or quoted trade price and the price at which the trade is actually executed.

=== Price impact ===
Price impact is the change in quoted or execution prices caused by a trade itself through its consumption of available liquidity or movement of market state.

=== Oracle (blockchain) ===
A blockchain oracle is a mechanism or service that supplies data or results from outside a blockchain's native state to smart contracts or other on-chain logic.

=== Attestation ===
An attestation is a statement or assertion made by an identifiable attester about a subject, event, property, or piece of data, often accompanied by evidence that allows others to verify who made the assertion and whether it was altered.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
blockchain ≠ cryptocurrency
blockchain ≠ every distributed ledger
smart contract ≠ legal contract by definition
token ≠ cryptocurrency by definition
wallet ≠ on-chain asset storage
DID ≠ person
zero knowledge ≠ application anonymity
DeFi ≠ total decentralization
AMM ≠ liquidity pool
AMM ≠ constant-product mechanism only
slippage ≠ price impact
oracle ≠ truth
attestation ≠ truth
</pre>

